  "notes": "kcal, protein, sodium, calcium, serving mass and food type for every item come from NASA's ISS Standard Menu Nutritional Data table (Appendix A of the NASA STEMonstration 'Nutrition' educator guide; PDF uploaded to nasa.gov in March 2023, table itself undated). Values were transcribed by hand from the rendered PDF pages. Category (Breakfast/Entrées/Sides/Snacks/Drinks) is our own grouping; NASA's table is alphabetical. For Rehydratable and Beverage items the NASA mass is small (e.g. coffee 1.5 g), consistent with dry weight as packaged before water is added; the table itself only says 'Mass - in grams'. NASA's table has no vitamin D column. Vitamin D comes from a 2017 NASA JSC presentation (NTRS 20170001326) where listed (8 items; IU converted at 40 IU = 1 µg; the slide gives one value per item and compares the menu total to a 600 IU/day requirement, so values are read as per serving), otherwise from USDA FoodData Central SR Legacy (April 2018 release, read from the official FDC CSV download because the DEMO_KEY API hit its rate limit). USDA values are per 100 g and were scaled to the NASA serving: by mass where the USDA food is in the same state as the NASA item (ready-to-eat, or a dry mix/powder for dry NASA items), or by energy (NASA kcal ÷ USDA kcal per 100 g) where the NASA item is dry but the USDA match is prepared (shrimp cocktail, green beans). A USDA vitamin D of 0 is the database's reported value for an Earth analog, not a measurement of the space food. NASA has noted that processing and storage reduce some vitamins, so actual flight values may be lower.",
